1	// The deterministic controller's scheduler: it selects ready work, assembles the team each stage needs (through the
2	// stage executors, which seat by capability through Pantheon's selector), and moves every eligible item through the
3	// governed lifecycle. No person selects tickets: `tick` dispatches whatever reconciliation finds ready, and
4	// `watch` runs a tick whenever the inputs change (the status ledger head, the work ledger head, the tickets, the
5	// published issues), so new work and dependency changes reach dispatch on their own.
6	//
7	// Bounds, declared before dispatch and enforced across the whole tick: total provider calls (recorded usage;
8	// a stage whose usage is unknown counts at its claim's budget, never as zero), iterations (stage attempts),
9	// elapsed time, retries per item and stage, and concurrency. A duplicate claim is refused by the work ledger
10	// itself. An interrupted claim (lease expired) is released as `expired` and the next tick resumes from the last
11	// recorded stage, reusing every stage that is still valid: nothing completed is repeated, no lineage is lost.
12	//
13	// A stage executor receives the VERIFIED handoff and returns its record. Executors are injected (stages.mjs builds
14	// the real ones over Pantheon; tests pass scripted ones). Missing authority (no controller key) or a missing
15	// executor produces a recorded block or a refusal, never a skipped verification.
